<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/foundational/the-renaissance-and-reformation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The Renaissance (c. 1350–1600) was not a light switch from "dark" to "bright" — it was
    <strong>wealth, recovered texts and new technology compounding in the cities of
    Italy</strong>, then spilling across Europe. Its twin, the Reformation (1517–), used the
    printing press to shatter religious unity. Together they made the modern West's politics,
    science and sense of individuality possible — at the price of a century and a half of war.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four drivers, from banking to Bible printing" widget="equationStep" config={renCfg} />

  <h2>The people and the politics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The giants:</strong> Leonardo (artist-engineer), Michelangelo (Sistine, David), Raphael, Titian; Brunelleschi's dome (1436) as engineering landmark; Machiavelli writing the handbook of modern politics (1513).</li>
    <li><strong>Humanism:</strong> the intellectual program — study classical texts and human potential. "Man is the measure of all things" (Protagoras, revived) — the philosophical pivot to modern individualism.</li>
    <li><strong>Printing (1450s) is the real protagonist:</strong> by 1500, ~20 million books in print. Luther's tracts sold 300,000 copies in three years — the Reformation was history's first media campaign, and the Church could not out-print it.</li>
    <li><strong>The costs:</strong> the Italian Wars, the sack of Rome (1527), witch persecutions, and the Thirty Years' War (1618–48) that killed a third of Germany — the modern state (sovereignty, tolerance as necessity) was built on the wreckage.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Burke, The Italian Renaissance; Eisenstein, The Printing Press as an Agent of Change; MacCulloch, The Reformation.</p>
</TopicPage>
